1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,095
Every NBA free throw

2
00:00:01,095 --> 00:00:02,509
is shot from 15 feet…

3
00:00:02,509 --> 00:00:03,490
except it isn't.

4
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:04,539
You see, the rulebook

5
00:00:04,539 --> 00:00:05,269
measures that line

6
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:06,044
from the backboard,

7
00:00:06,044 --> 00:00:06,843
not from the rim.

8
00:00:06,843 --> 00:00:07,949
The rim sits 6 inches

9
00:00:07,949 --> 00:00:08,656
off the glass,

10
00:00:08,656 --> 00:00:10,081
and it's 18 inches wide,

11
00:00:10,081 --> 00:00:10,652
so the middle

12
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:11,119
of the hoop

13
00:00:11,119 --> 00:00:11,872
is 15 inches

14
00:00:11,872 --> 00:00:12,852
in front of the backboard.

15
00:00:12,852 --> 00:00:14,119
Which means every free throw

16
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:15,271
is really 13 feet,

17
00:00:15,271 --> 00:00:15,886
9 inches.

18
00:00:15,886 --> 00:00:16,901
But here's the weird part.

19
00:00:16,901 --> 00:00:17,916
The three-point line

20
00:00:17,916 --> 00:00:18,623
IS measured from

21
00:00:18,623 --> 00:00:19,730
the middle of the basket.

22
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:20,847
So the two most important

23
00:00:20,847 --> 00:00:21,691
lines in basketball

24
00:00:21,691 --> 00:00:22,352
are measured from

25
00:00:22,352 --> 00:00:23,299
two different places.

26
00:00:23,299 --> 00:00:24,269
And some of the biggest guys

27
00:00:24,269 --> 00:00:24,713
in the league

28
00:00:24,713 --> 00:00:25,443
still can't make it

29
00:00:25,443 --> 00:00:26,640
from 13 feet 9.
